# 8. OUTPUT FORMAT: TIMESHEET GRID

Generate the timesheet using the same 12-column structure and formatting as our previous timesheet outputs.

## 8.1 Required Column Order

The columns must remain in this exact order:

`Log ID | Date | Start Time | End Time | Duration (hh:mm:ss) | Client | Project | Remarks | Status | Created At | Last Updated | Time Basis`

Do not reorder, rename, remove, or add columns.

## 8.2 Formatting Requirements

- Output a copy-paste-ready TSV (tab-separated values) inside a code block.
- Do not use a Markdown table.
- Include the header row.
- Every row must contain exactly 12 columns.
- Keep Log ID, Created At, and Last Updated blank unless actual values are provided.
- Use full datetime format: `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`.
- Use organic, varied timestamps. Avoid repetitive or perfectly rounded time patterns.
- Do not use email timestamps as work start or end times.
- Do not create overlapping task times.
- Preserve exact task durations when provided by the source.
- When task durations or time allocations need to be determined, use reasonable allocations based on the work described and the applicable work schedule.
- Do not present estimated times as source-recorded actual times.
- Put any estimation disclosure in the final Time Basis column, not in Remarks.
- Keep Remarks concise and focused on the actual task performed.
- Do not add unnecessary columns, explanations, or formatting inside the TSV.

## 8.3 Timestamp and Duration Rules

- Use the applicable client's working schedule and time zone.
- Do not infer actual start or end times from email timestamps.
- If actual task times are provided, preserve them.
- If only the shift duration is known, allocate reasonable task intervals based on the EOD description.
- Ensure task intervals are chronological and non-overlapping.
- Ensure Duration equals End Time minus Start Time.
- Use `hh:mm:ss` for duration values.
- Do not create artificial precision that suggests estimated values were recorded automatically.
- If exact timestamps cannot be established, use reasonable estimated allocations and disclose the estimation in Time Basis.
- Do not exceed the available shift duration unless the source explicitly confirms additional working time.

## 8.4 Time Basis Column

Use this column to distinguish the source of the time information.

Examples:

- `Actual - source provided`
- `Estimated - allocated from 4-hour shift`
- `Estimated - based on task scope`
- `Partial actual / partial estimated`

Do not place estimation disclosures in Remarks.

## 8.5 TSV Example

Use this only as a structural example. Do not copy its values into actual timesheets.

```tsv
Log ID	Date	Start Time	End Time	Duration (hh:mm:ss)	Client	Project	Remarks	Status	Created At	Last Updated	Time Basis
	2026-11-18 00:00:00	2026-11-18 10:12:00	2026-11-18 10:48:00	00:36:00	[Approved Client]	[Approved Project]	Reviewed pending project requirements	Completed			Estimated - allocated from 4-hour shift
	2026-11-18 00:00:00	2026-11-18 10:51:00	2026-11-18 12:16:00	01:25:00	[Approved Client]	[Approved Project]	Prepared and revised page content	In Progress			Estimated - based on task scope
```

## 8.6 Validation Summary

After the TSV code block, provide a brief validation summary stating:

- Number of entries
- Clients included
- Whether time allocations are actual, estimated, or mixed
- Any assumptions made
- Any tasks that could not be confidently classified

Do not add explanations inside the TSV itself.

If the grid cannot be generated accurately because essential information is missing, flag the issue instead of fabricating data.

